    {
      "date": "2026-10-02",
      "category": "Backend",
      "title": "3009: harden Web Push subscription and relay boundaries (Issue #26)",
      "description": "Subscribe now validates the endpoint (string <= 2048, no whitespace/control chars, valid https URL, no credentials, default port, DNS-name host only: no IP literal in any spelling, no localhost/single-label/.local/.internal), p256dh (unpadded canonical base64url, exactly 65 bytes) and auth (exactly 16 bytes) per RFC 8291, and expirationTime (null/omitted or a future integer ms); only endpoint, expirationTime and keys are stored. Dedup and the per-user bound are atomic: the old $pull-then-$push (which could duplicate under concurrent requests) is replaced by single-document conditional updates (refresh in place via the positional operator; else $ne-guarded $push with $each + $slice -10; else refresh if a concurrent request stored it), so duplicates are impossible and a user never holds more than 10 subscriptions; the newest wins and the oldest is dropped so legitimate users with stale endpoints can always register a new device. Unsubscribe requires a non-empty string endpoint <= 2048 (rejecting operator objects like { $ne } that previously reached the $pull and could delete all of a user's subscriptions); unknown endpoints remain a 200 no-op. Relay validates title (<= 100), body (<= 500) and tag (<= 200, default halotask-push, no control chars) plus a 3993-byte serialised payload cap, before anything else; limits are derived from the client's reminder payloads and RFC 8291. Delivery is independent per subscription with a 10 s idle socket timeout; DNS resolution has a separate cancellable 5 s hard deadline; response is { sent, failed, pruned } (failed is additive); 404/410 are pruned in one $pull, all other failures keep the subscription; a prune failure no longer turns a completed relay into a 500. Logs contain only the push host and status, never endpoints, keys, payloads or upstream error text. The #23 relay rate limiter is unchanged. Tests: 165 server tests in tests/pushRoutes.test.ts plus 97 in tests/pushNetworkGuard.test.ts (in-memory User model that emulates only the operations the controller should issue and throws on anything else, a gate that forces interleaving of concurrent requests, fake web-push); mutation checks on the dedup guard, bound, race retry, pruning, logging, timeout and each validator were caught, and two mutants (removing the in-place-refresh fast path; the explicit IPv4-mapped unwrap, which Node's BlockList already does) are behaviourally equivalent. SSRF (review follow-up): shape checks cannot see DNS resolution, so delivery now passes web-push an https.Agent (utils/pushNetworkGuard.ts) whose DNS lookup refuses any non-public address (loopback, RFC1918, CGNAT, link-local/metadata, reserved, ULA/link-local/multicast/NAT64/6to4 IPv6, IPv4-mapped IPv6; a mixed answer fails as a whole); the validated address is the connected address, defeating DNS rebinding. Provider-agnostic on purpose: no hostname allowlist (browsers produce fcm.googleapis.com, updates.push.services.mozilla.com, web.push.apple.com, *.notify.windows.com, and an exact or suffix list would break others or be a broad invented one); all existing shape checks are unchanged. Added root `npm run verify` (client: test+build; server: test, typecheck, typecheck:tests, build; stops on first failure); the server test script is unchanged and still includes the Mongo-backed suite. DNS time bound (follow-up): web-push's timeout is an idle socket timeout started before the lookup finishes, so a never-completing DNS lookup is still settled by it, but dns.lookup (getaddrinfo) cannot be cancelled and would pin a libuv threadpool thread; the guard's default resolver is now c-ares (dns.Resolver: 2 s x 2 tries per query, hard 5 s deadline, cancel() on expiry) so a stalled name leaves no work running; A+AAAA are resolved once and only the validated addresses reach the socket. The 10 s figure is an idle timeout, not a total deadline. Tests: 97 in tests/pushNetworkGuard.test.ts (silent and answering fake UDP DNS servers, real web-push with a never-settling resolver) and 165 in tests/pushRoutes.test.ts; mutation checks on cancel, deadline, per-attempt timeout, default constants and timer cleanup were caught. Trade-off: c-ares ignores /etc/hosts. Known limits: an attacker-controlled public hostname is still accepted and receives a fixed encrypted blind POST; legacy over-limit entries are trimmed only on the next new subscription; Mongo operator semantics ($ positional update, $ne on an array path, $push with $slice) are verified only against the emulation because tests/api.routes.test.ts still needs MongoMemoryServer, which cannot download its binary in the sandbox.",
      "files": [
