    {
      "date": "2026-10-01",
      "category": "Bugfix",
      "title": "3009: fix History source of truth, date semantics, and sync reliability (Issue #31)",
      "description": "Client history lived only in one global IndexedDB key (task_history) shared by every account on the browser, getWeekHistory() never read the server so a cleared IndexedDB lost history even though snapshots had been pushed, failed pushes were swallowed and never retried, and the server built its GET window with toISOString() (UTC) while the client used local calendar days. The server also accepted any YYYY-MM-DD on PUT /api/history/today, coerced numbers with Number(x) || 0 and stored completedTasks with no shape or bounds. Model: the history of a day is the SET of completed tasks keyed by task id; count and minutes are recomputed from it, so reconciling cannot double-count. The server is the durable source of truth; IndexedDB (now per user: task_history:<userId>) is the offline cache and outbox. Every local change and the first read of a session run pull -> merge -> push: a pending local snapshot is merged into the server row as (server - tasks this device un-completed) U local by task id and is never allowed to replace the server row wholesale, so a device that was offline cannot erase tasks another device completed (server A,B,C + pending local A,B stays A,B,C and nothing is sent). A task is removed only when this device itself saw it stop being completed. pendingSync clears only when the exact pushed revision is acknowledged, transient failures are retried (throttled), a payload 400 is permanent but a DATE_* 400 is retried, bursts of edits coalesce, snapshots are applied in request order (fixed a race where the one-time legacy purge let a later snapshot jump the lock), and a push is skipped if the account changed. No timestamps are compared, so clock skew cannot reorder snapshots. Calendar days: ONE convention. A history date is the user's local calendar day = the calendar date of an instant shifted by the user's UTC offset. The client declares utcOffsetMinutes (-720..840) on every history request; the server validates 'today' from its clock plus that offset with NO clock-skew tolerance (exactly one date: yesterday and tomorrow are rejected; failures carry codes DATE_NOT_TODAY / DATE_OUT_OF_RANGE, which the client treats as retryable while other 400s stay permanent) and has no default offset and no UTC fallback (GET requires endDate and utcOffsetMinutes; calls without them are rejected). API: PUT /today accepts only today at the declared offset; PUT /:date is a bounded backfill (last 7 days, never future) required because snapshots are queued and pushed after their own day has ended (call path: reconcile -> pushEntry -> date === today ? PUT /today : PUT /:date). GET days must be a single integer 1-90 (rejected, not clamped); repeated/structured params, non-finite/negative/excessive numbers, malformed or duplicate completedTasks, oversized arrays and invalid/future dates are rejected with 400 (utils/historyValidators.ts); GET returns updatedAt (null for days with no record). Legacy: the old global task_history key has no provable owner, so it is discarded, never read, merged, pushed or assigned to whichever user logs in next; it is deleted on first use and server-persisted history is recoverable. Tests: 62 client history tests and 223 server history tests (Mongo-independent: mocked model with the real router and auth), including merge-by-id cases, a 1024-case union property test, UTC+14, UTC-12, India, Los Angeles (PDT and PST), DST transitions, midnight boundaries (cross-checked against Intl), legacy purge and user isolation. Growth Tree reward rules and the Growth Tree's own UTC streak date are unchanged. Known limits: tests/api.routes.test.ts needs MongoMemoryServer, which cannot download a binary in the sandbox; two devices that pull and push within the same short GET-PUT gap can still overwrite each other (closing it needs an atomic server-side merge, untestable here); the global tasks cache (offline/cache.ts) has the same cross-user bug class and is not addressed.",
      "files": [
        "halotasks-client/src/offline/history.ts",
        "halotasks-client/src/services/historyService.ts",
        "halotasks-client/src/utils/localDate.ts",
        "halotasks-client/src/offline/history.test.ts",
        "halotasks-server/src/controllers/history.controller.ts",
        "halotasks-server/src/routes/history.routes.ts",
        "halotasks-server/src/models/DayHistory.model.ts",
        "halotasks-server/src/utils/calendarDate.ts",
        "halotasks-server/src/utils/historyValidators.ts",
        "halotasks-server/tests/historyValidators.test.ts",
        "halotasks-server/tests/historyRoutes.test.ts",
        "docs/context.md"
      ],
      "priority": "high",
      "status": "completed"
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-30",
      "category": "Bugfix",
      "title": "3009: isolate Growth Tree client state per authenticated user (Issue #30)",
      "description": "Fixed an account-to-account contamination bug in the client Growth Tree persistence layer: state lived under one global IndexedDB key (growth_tree) with an owner-less module-level cache, so after logout/login on a shared browser user A's local XP and awardedTaskIds could be merged into user B's state and pushed to B's server record. treeStorage.ts now keys IndexedDB records as growth_tree:<userId> (user id from useAuthStore, via the existing offlineDb; no schema change) and the in-memory cache records its owner, so getTreeState() never returns another user's state. initTreeStorage() throws TreeIdentityError when there is no authenticated user and discards its result (no cache write, IndexedDB write or server push) if the user changes while it is awaiting local or server state; setTreeState() throws unless init has completed for the current user. Legacy unscoped data (growth_tree in IndexedDB, halotask:growth_tree in localStorage) has no provable owner, so it is never read or migrated and is deleted on init; the server copy is the recovery path for progress that was successfully synchronized. clearAuth() intentionally does not clear Growth Tree state: owner-scoped keys and cache already prevent cross-user reads, and wiping on logout or 401 would destroy the previous user's unsynced offline progress. useDashboardGrowth re-initialises when the user id changes and skips awards on identity mismatch; InsightsPage now handles an init rejection explicitly (logs and shows no tree while the rest of the page loads). Reward rules, XP values, streak/health rules, stage thresholds and merge semantics are unchanged. Added 13 regression tests (two users on shared storage, logout/login cycles, higher local XP vs lower server XP, awardedTaskIds isolation, mid-init account switch, no-user failure, legacy purge, single-user/offline/merge behaviour). Full client suite (29 tests), tsc -b and vite build pass. Known follow-up: the global tasks cache in offline/cache.ts has the same cross-user bug class and is not addressed here.",
      "files": [
        "halotasks-client/src/growth/treeStorage.ts",
        "halotasks-client/src/growth/treeStorage.test.ts",
        "halotasks-client/src/hooks/useDashboardGrowth.ts",
        "halotasks-client/src/pages/InsightsPage.tsx",
        "docs/context.md"
      ],
      "priority": "high",
      "status": "completed"
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-30",
      "category": "Backend",
      "title": "3009: harden server startup, shutdown, CORS, and request limits",
      "description": "startServer() now has an explicit .catch() (previously `void startServer();` with no rejection handling), so a startup-specific failure like connectDB() rejecting gets a clear \"Failed to start server\" log and a non-zero exit instead of silently becoming an unhandled rejection. Extracted resolveOrigin() (CORS) into src/config/cors.ts and the startup-failure/graceful-shutdown logic into src/utils/processLifecycle.ts (exitWithFatalError(), createGracefulShutdown()) as standalone, dependency-injected functions so they're unit-testable without spawning a real process or opening a real HTTP/DB connection; behavior is unchanged (CORS still fails closed in production when CLIENT_ORIGIN is missing, dev still falls back to localhost, shutdown still closes the HTTP server before Mongoose with a 10s unref'd force-exit safety timer). Fixed a real bug found while extracting this: the global error handler in app.ts flattened every error to a generic 500, including body-parser's own express.json() errors for an oversized body (413, PayloadTooLargeError) or malformed JSON (400, SyntaxError) - both were being reported as 500 even though body-parser already knew the correct status. Added src/utils/httpErrors.ts (isPayloadTooLargeError(), isMalformedJsonBodyError()) with type guards verified empirically against the installed body-parser version, and the error handler now checks those first. Added unit tests for resolveOrigin(), exitWithFatalError()/createGracefulShutdown() (including force-exit-on-timeout, no-double-exit-on-a-second-signal, and resilience to closeServer/closeDb errors), and the httpErrors guards - none need MongoDB. Added tests/httpBoundary.test.ts, a supertest-based integration suite exercising the real CORS/body-limit/error-handler wiring end to end (also without MongoDB, by building a small app from the same middleware/resolver rather than importing src/app.ts directly, which would otherwise trigger a Mongoose \"Cannot overwrite model\" error when re-imported under different env vars in the same test run). Documented the 1MB request body limit and the CORS fail-closed behavior in README.md and docs/context.md.",
      "files": [
        "halotasks-server/src/config/cors.ts",
        "halotasks-server/src/utils/processLifecycle.ts",
        "halotasks-server/src/utils/httpErrors.ts",
        "halotasks-server/src/app.ts",
        "halotasks-server/src/server.ts",
        "halotasks-server/tests/cors.test.ts",
        "halotasks-server/tests/processLifecycle.test.ts",
        "halotasks-server/tests/httpErrors.test.ts",
        "halotasks-server/tests/httpBoundary.test.ts",
        "README.md",
        "docs/context.md"
      ]
    },
    {
      "date": "2026-09-26",
      "category": "Backend",
      "title": "2609: harden task API validation and query boundaries",
      "description": "Malformed task ids on PUT/DELETE now return 400 instead of a 500 from an unhandled Mongoose CastError. Task body validation (title, description, tags, priority, dueDate, estimatedMinutes) is centralized in taskValidators.ts and applied identically on create and update, so an empty/whitespace title or an invalid due date is rejected consistently regardless of endpoint (previously update silently dropped an empty title instead of rejecting it). Added length/count limits: title 200 chars, description 2000 chars, up to 20 tags of 50 chars each, estimatedMinutes 0-100000. GET /api/tasks is now paginated (default and max page size 200) with page/limit/total/hasMore added additively to the response. Fixed a real backward-compatibility gap found in review: the frontend's taskService.getTasks() previously assumed a single page had every task, which would have silently truncated any user with >200 tasks; it now transparently loops pages until hasMore is false (capped at 500 pages as a runaway-loop guard), so the paginated backend is invisible to the rest of the app. Also fixed estimatedMinutes accepting an empty string as 0 (Number('') === 0), tightened the pagination parser to use Number.isSafeInteger and to reject repeated/array query params (?page=2&page=999) instead of silently taking the first value. findOneAndUpdate now runs schema validators. Added unit tests for the validators/pagination parser/taskService pagination-consumption and integration tests for malformed ids, invalid dates, empty/over-length titles, invalid priority, tag limits, estimatedMinutes bounds (including the empty-string case), and pagination boundaries including ownership filtering. Follow-up from review: the client's 500-page safety ceiling now throws an explicit error instead of returning a silently truncated task list, and the aggregated result reports hasMore: false so its pagination metadata is internally consistent.",
      "files": [
        "halotasks-server/src/controllers/task.controller.ts",
        "halotasks-server/src/utils/taskValidators.ts",
        "halotasks-server/src/utils/pagination.ts",
        "halotasks-server/tests/taskValidators.test.ts",
        "halotasks-server/tests/pagination.test.ts",
        "halotasks-server/tests/api.routes.test.ts",
        "halotasks-client/src/services/taskService.ts",
        "halotasks-client/src/services/taskService.test.ts",
        "halotasks-client/src/types/task.ts",
        "halotasks-client/src/hooks/useDashboardSync.test.ts",
        "docs/context.md"
      ],
      "priority": "high",
      "status": "completed"
    },
